<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khan Ahmad Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Khan Ahmad Khan Gilani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khan Ahmad Khan was the last ruler of the Karkiya dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahijan">Lahijan</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>, on the Caspian coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Long held prisoner by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp I</a>, he was restored and later quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> over marriage alliances and his dealings with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Abbas invaded Gilan in 1592, Khan Ahmad fled to the Ottoman Empire, and Gilan was annexed to Safavid crown lands.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khan Ahmad Khan was an infant when his father, Soltan-Hasan Kiya, died of plague in 1538, and he was crowned king of the Karkiya dynasty of Bia-pish, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>. He was soon given Bia-pas, western Gilan, as well, but the brutality of the Karkiya army there led its people to invite Amira Shahrok, a distant relative of the former ruler Mozaffar Soltan, to rule them. Khan Ahmad later persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp I</a> to have Amira Shahrok executed, and had his successor Soltan Mahmud poisoned in exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, so becoming sole ruler of Gilan again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tahmasp, wary of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> who had not come to court for 20 years, made Jamshid Khan ruler of Bia-pas and ordered Khan Ahmad to return Kuchesfahan to Amira Sasan. Khan Ahmad refused and rebelled. In June 1567 his commander Shah Mansur Lahiji defeated Amira Sasan near Siah-rudbar, and the shah's envoy Yulqoli Beg Zu'l-Qadr was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>. Qizilbash troops then defeated his army under Kiya Rostam, plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> in their search for him and captured him. He was held in Qahqaheh Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, where he befriended Tahmasp's son Ismail, and then for 10 years in a fortress at Estakhr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>.</p><p>Ismail II ordered his release, but the order was not carried out. His successor Mohammad Khodabanda freed him at the proposal of his queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khayr-al-nisa-begum">Khayr al-Nisa Begum</a>, a close relative of Khan Ahmad, gave him his sister Maryam Begum in marriage and restored him in Bia-pish. Fifteen years of fierce conflict followed. Jamshid Khan routed his first invasion of Bia-pas and stacked the captives' heads into the shape of a minaret, but Khan Ahmad invaded several more times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1591 Shah Abbas asked for Khan Ahmad's daughter Yakhan Begum as wife for his son Mohammad Baqer Mirza, since Khan Ahmad had no male heir; Khan Ahmad refused because of her age. This and economic motives brought a Safavid invasion. Abbas destroyed his palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahijan">Lahijan</a>, and Khan Ahmad spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> trying in vain to regain power. He died in 1596 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>. In 1594/5 the grand vizier Hatem Beg Ordubadi reformed the province's taxes, most likely because of the wealth of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, caviar and lumber.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khan Ahmad Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Khan Ahmad Khan</b>, the last ruler, quarrelled with Shah Abbas I, who conquered Gilan in 1592; Khan Ahmad fled to the Ottoman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/karkiya-dynasty">Karkiya dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahijan">Lahijan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.21,50&amp;year=1564" aria-label="Lahijan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5802 824 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Khan Ahmad Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6252 1094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6252 1094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.21,50&amp;year=1564">What was here in 1564? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
